# Respin environment template — copy to .env.local and fill in values there.
# (.env* is gitignored; this template is named env.example, not .env.example,
# because the repo's guardrails deny writes to .env.* paths wholesale.)
#
# THIS FILE IS THE ENV AUTHORITY, AND A TEST HOLDS IT TO THAT (R-155, register
# 2026-10-05 item 14). `tests/env-example.test.ts` derives every variable the
# app, the worker, lib/, the scripts and the operator shell scripts read, and
# fails unless each one is named here (a `NAME=` line, commented or not) or sits
# on its written exemption list with a reason (runtime-set, script-internal,
# deliberately ignored...). It also fails on a name here that nothing reads. So a
# new reader is red until it is documented here.

# Postgres connection string (R-18: self-hosted Postgres — Docker locally,
# Lightsail in production). For local dev with the bundled docker-compose.yml
# this exact value works (throwaway local-only credentials, port 5435):
# postgres://respin:respin_local_dev@localhost:5435/respin
DATABASE_URL=
# `pnpm db:seed` inserts a fake dev user, so it refuses a DATABASE_URL whose
# host is not local. Exactly `1` overrides that, deliberately, for a remote DEV
# database. Never set it against production.
# RESPIN_SEED_FORCE=

# Better Auth (R-19). Secret: any long random string — generate one with
#   openssl rand -base64 32
# and never commit it.
BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=
# The app's own base URL. Local dev: http://localhost:8000 (the `dev` script
# pins `next dev -p 8000`; this value MUST match it or Better Auth rejects the
# session and Stripe's return URLs point at a port nothing is serving).
# ALSO used to build Stripe Checkout's success/cancel return URLs (M1): with it
# unset, subscribe and pack purchase refuse rather than guess a host.
BETTER_AUTH_URL=

# Which upstream hops may be trusted to report the client IP (R-26/R-27).
# REQUIRED in every non-local environment — staging and preview too, not just
# production: Better Auth's localhost fallback covers only development/dev/test.
# Without it every auth request fails with a 500 (the process still starts), and
# the error prints these same two options.
#
# Better Auth resolves the client IP from `x-forwarded-for` to key the sign-in
# rate limiter. With no trusted proxies it trusts only a SINGLE-value header,
# so behind a two-hop proxy every request collapses onto ONE shared bucket and
# a single client can exhaust the 5-per-minute sign-in limit for everybody.
#
#   RESPIN_TRUSTED_PROXIES=10.0.0.0/8,172.31.0.0/16   # real proxy IPs/CIDRs
#   RESPIN_TRUSTED_PROXIES=none                       # single-hop: opt out
#
# Do NOT guess the addresses: a wrong value lets a client spoof its own IP and
# evade the limiter entirely. Set it from the deployment's actual proxy hops,
# and use the NARROWEST ranges that cover them — a range broad enough to also
# contain real client IPs makes every hop "trusted", which resolves no client at
# all and lands you back in the one-shared-bucket state (R-27). The runtime tell
# is Better Auth's one-time "could not determine a client IP" warning.
RESPIN_TRUSTED_PROXIES=

# Comma-separated Better Auth user ids allowed into /admin. FAIL CLOSED: empty
# or unset denies everyone. Find your id in the `user` table after signing up.
# You need this to reach /admin/config, which is where the Stripe price map
# from `pnpm stripe:setup` gets pasted (see README, Services step 4.3).
ADMIN_USER_IDS=
# Comma-separated Better Auth user ids left out of the activation counts on
# /admin/activation and the daily PostHog aggregate, beside ADMIN_USER_IDS
# (which are always left out). For the founder's and testers' own accounts.
# Ids only — never an email-domain or name rule (R-121). Unset = none.
ACTIVATION_EXCLUDED_USER_IDS=

# --- Optional: Google sign-in (owner-created OAuth client; email/password
# --- works without these). Google Cloud console → Credentials → OAuth client.
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=

# --- Billing (M1). Everything below is TEST MODE for local development.
# Stripe secret key (test mode): dashboard → Developers → API keys.
# Absent = the app still builds and runs, /usage and /settings/billing still
# render, and every billing control is DISABLED with the remedy printed beside
# it rather than throwing when clicked; the underlying actions refuse with a
# named error (StripeNotConfiguredError), and `pnpm stripe:setup` prints how
# to supply it. Never commit a live key.
STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=
# Webhook signing secret. `stripe listen --forward-to
# localhost:8000/api/stripe/webhook` prints one (whsec_...) when it starts.
# Absent = the webhook endpoint returns 500 rather than trusting a payload.
STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# Immutable key id v1. Back up this secret outside PostgreSQL; paid Stripe
# events may need it after a point-in-time database restore.
RESPIN_AUTO_TOPUP_AUTHORITY_KEY=
# Explicit cutover binding. Get the account id from Stripe; set mode to exactly
# true in production or false in test/dev. Rollout refuses a mismatched key.
RESPIN_STRIPE_ACCOUNT_ID=
RESPIN_STRIPE_LIVEMODE=false
# `pnpm stripe:setup` loads `respin/.env.local` by default; this names a
# different file instead (a path that does not exist means "load nothing",
# which is how its tests exercise the missing-key refusal). Optional.
# RESPIN_ENV_FILE=

# --- The model provider (M2b slice 2a). The FIRST secret whose absence stops a
# creator-facing action rather than an operator one.
# Anthropic API key: console.anthropic.com → API keys. Absent = the metered run
# on /onboarding refuses by name (LlmNotConfiguredError, which names this
# variable and never its value); nothing else on the product degrades, and no
# credit is spent. The adapter pins its origin to https://api.anthropic.com at
# the socket, so this key is only ever sent there.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
# NOT read: ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL. The SDK would read it, so the adapter passes
# its pinned origin explicitly and the variable has no effect (R2).
#
# LEAVE UNSET OUTSIDE THE END-TO-END HARNESS. The model-transport selector the
# journeys use to swap in their transport fake (R-151). Unset or blank = the
# real provider. Any value but the fake's exact name refuses server start, and
# the fake's name is refused in a production build and against any database
# whose name is not `respin_e2e_*` / `respin_test_*`.
# RESPIN_LLM_TRANSPORT=

# --- The run-slot ceiling (tech-spec 6 / R-39). OPTIONAL: leave it unset and
# the process defaults to 16.
# How many model calls this PROCESS may have in flight at once, across every
# workspace. It is the size of a pool of its own -- slots are session advisory
# locks, which means a connection held for the whole vendor call, so they are
# deliberately NOT taken from the query pool (a slot holding the connection its
# own debit needs is a deadlock nothing recovers from). At this ceiling a run is
# REFUSED with "we are at capacity", never queued.
#
# PER-TIER limits are config, not env: they belong to the versioned document an
# operator edits at /admin/config. This one is env because it is a property of
# the machine, not of a customer's plan. An unparseable value is refused at
# call time rather than silently defaulted -- a ceiling quietly ignored is an
# unbounded vendor spend.
#
# WHAT TO DO WHEN REFUSALS START (the two look alike and the fixes differ):
#   `run_slot_busy` in the logs      -> ONE WORKSPACE is at its tier's limit.
#                                       Raise `concurrencyLimits.<tier>` at
#                                       /admin/config. No deploy, no restart.
#   `server_at_capacity` in the logs -> THE PROCESS is at its ceiling, whoever
#                                       is using it. Raise this variable and
#                                       restart -- but read the footprint first.
#
# THE FOOTPRINT, because raising this blindly runs out of CONNECTIONS rather
# than slots: one app process holds up to 10 (query pool) + RUN_SLOT_POOL_MAX
# (slot pool) Postgres connections. At the default 16 that is 26 per process
# against Postgres's default max_connections of 100, so three processes fit
# with room for psql and the migration CLI. A fourth does not, and the symptom
# of overshooting is MORE `server_at_capacity`, pointing at the wrong number.
RUN_SLOT_POOL_MAX=

# --- Dedicated pg-boss worker (Slice 8 / R-52). All are optional machine
# ceilings; invalid values make the worker refuse startup. The production
# command is `pnpm worker:start`, never the Next.js process.
#
# CONNECTION FOOTPRINT: queue pool (default 2) + pg-boss's one dedicated
# LISTEN/NOTIFY session + query pool (default 1) = 4 worker connections. The
# "+1 listener" half is MEASURED, not assumed: tests/pg-boss.docker.test.ts
# starts with `useListenNotify: true` and asserts total sessions equal pooled
# sessions plus exactly one at peak, for the proof boss and for the production
# runtime's own constructor options. Both configured pool values are clamped
# to a code ceiling of 2. Add this to each app process's 26-connection
# ceiling before changing either number.
RESPIN_WORKER_QUEUE_POOL_MAX=
RESPIN_WORKER_QUERY_POOL_MAX=

# Paid autopsy execution and its bounded in-process wait queue. Defaults are 4
# active / 16 waiting; code ceilings are 8 / 32. Raising these does not raise
# either database pool and therefore can increase query wait rather than work.
RESPIN_WORKER_CONCURRENCY=
RESPIN_WORKER_LOCAL_QUEUE_MAX=

# Content-free health snapshot interval in milliseconds; default 30000.
RESPIN_WORKER_HEARTBEAT_MS=

# Deletion REQUEST enablement (Phase 10b-1 rollout). Comma-separated closed
# list of scopes a signed-in person may REQUEST deletion for from
# /settings/account: `identity`, `profile`, `workspace`. Unset or blank = no
# scope accepts a request, which is the launch state ("deploy additively with
# deletion request creation disabled"); the page renders a closed scope as
# closed. Cancelling an in-flight deletion is NEVER gated by this flag, so
# rollback can clear it without stranding an operation. An unknown token
# refuses the request rather than enabling nothing or everything. The
# irreversible half is the WORKER flag below; both are needed for a deletion
# to run end to end.
#
# PUBLIC COPY IS TIED TO THIS FLAG (audit P1-A1, gate L5). `/legal` and the
# changelog's 2026-09-08 entry say deletion requests are NOT open on this
# service yet — fixed text, written for this flag being blank. Setting a scope
# here without rewriting both sentences makes that copy false. Phase 5 of the
# audit-remediation plan derives both from this flag; until then
# `tests/marketing-claims.test.tsx` reads THIS line and goes red if it names a
# scope while the not-yet-open copy stands.
RESPIN_DELETION_REQUEST_SCOPES=

# Deletion erasure enablement (Phase 10b-1 Task 4, R-119 rollout). Comma-
# separated closed list of scopes whose IRREVERSIBLE erasure the worker may run
# once an operation's grace window has passed: `identity`, `profile`,
# `workspace`. Unset or blank = none — every operation holds at `grace`, which
# is the launch state. An unknown token refuses worker startup, and so does
# EVERY scope today, with the executor's own reason: `workspace` and `profile`
# while the financial chain still cascades (R-122: ledger, subscription, pause
# and model-usage rows — Task 6's seven-year receiver), `identity` while the
# raw Stripe payload that names a workspace's contact has no receiver (also
# Task 6). Nothing can leave its request state at all until the deletion
# journal below is configured.
RESPIN_DELETION_ERASURE_SCOPES=

# Public Sample Spin (Phase 10a, R-116/R-117/R-123). Unset or blank = disabled:
# the landing page keeps its illustrative mockup and POST /api/demo answers
# 404. `preview` = the real, metered Sample Spin is live on this deployment
# (the fictional fixture brain, the production analyseAndSpin pipeline, one
# admitted request per client-IP bucket per 24 h, two concurrent product-wide,
# $10/day purpose cap inside the $100/day system-spend ceiling). `public` is
# NOT a value this build accepts — only Phase 10c may open it, after the dated
# platform registry and the privacy text pass. Any other value refuses.
RESPIN_PUBLIC_SAMPLE_SPIN=

# The dedicated, VERSIONED key the Sample Spin buckets visitors under:
# HMAC-SHA256(key, canonical client IP), keyed by version. Format
# `v1=<64+ hex chars>` — the current key — optionally followed by
# `;v0=<hex>`, the immediately prior key kept ONLY until the last bucket opened
# under it expires (at most 24 h after rotation; `pnpm sample-spin:keyring`
# exits 0 when it may be erased, 3 while a bucket under it is live). Never
# BETTER_AUTH_SECRET, never a
# key any other purpose uses. Generate with
# `node -e "console.log('v1='+require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"`.
# Required when RESPIN_PUBLIC_SAMPLE_SPIN=preview; the raw IP is never stored.
RESPIN_PUBLIC_SAMPLE_SPIN_HMAC_KEYS=

# --- Telemetry (Phase 10a plan C5; content-free, SDK-less). Unset = disabled;
# the app and worker run identically without them.
#
# Sentry: error EVENTS built only from safe-log's allowlist (refusal code, error
# class, driver SQLSTATE, route pattern) — never a message, request, header,
# cookie, user or body. 4,000 events/month per process, code-fixed.
SENTRY_DSN=
# A token tag on every event (`staging`, `production`, ...). Unset = `production`.
# A malformed value refuses process start rather than silently dropping events.
SENTRY_ENVIRONMENT=
# 0..1; may only LOWER the send rate (default 1). Invalid = refuses at start.
RESPIN_SENTRY_SAMPLE_RATE=
# PostHog: ONE daily aggregate event (`activation_cohort_matured`, counts only,
# cohorts under ten signups withheld) under the system identity, person
# processing off. Both or neither; the host must be https. 10,000 events/month
# per process, code-fixed. Exact counts are on /admin/activation regardless.
POSTHOG_HOST=
POSTHOG_PROJECT_KEY=

# --- The external deletion journal (Phase 10b-1 Task 5, R-124). The append-only
# S3 store that records every deletion transition OUTSIDE the database, so a
# database restore cannot roll a deletion back.
#
# ALL THREE OR NONE. A partial set refuses worker startup rather than starting a
# worker whose every journal append quietly fails -- which would look exactly
# like a deployment that configured no journal at all.
#
# UNSET is a supported, shipped state: the worker composes a refusing journal,
# every append returns a conflict, and no deletion operation can advance past
# the state its request left it in. That is the launch default and it is safe.
#
# Provisioning is an owner action and is NOT done by this app: a dedicated S3
# bucket with Versioning and Object Lock enabled BEFORE its first object, three
# least-privilege principals, and the bucket policy -- see
# infra/s3-deletion-journal/README.md. Deletion and public launch stay disabled
# until the bucket exists AND a real restore walk has been recorded.
RESPIN_DELETION_JOURNAL_BUCKET=
RESPIN_DELETION_JOURNAL_REGION=
# The key prefix, e.g. `production` or `staging`. Two deployments sharing one
# bucket MUST differ here, or their journals interleave under one prefix and a
# gap becomes undetectable.
RESPIN_DELETION_JOURNAL_ENVIRONMENT=
# Local S3-compatible endpoint for contract testing ONLY. Never set in
# production. Anything that is not https and not loopback is refused.
# The LOCAL DRILL uses exactly http://127.0.0.1:9000 (MinIO, `docker compose
# --profile drill`) — the IP form, so the SDK uses path-style addressing.
RESPIN_DELETION_JOURNAL_ENDPOINT=
# LOCAL DRILL ONLY — MinIO's throwaway root user, read by the AWS SDK's default
# credential chain (neither the journal writer nor the verifier passes keys).
# Never set in production: there the SDK resolves the instance role (T-23).
# The values are in respin/docker-compose.yml and RUNBOOK.md's local-drill block.
# AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=

# --- Transactional mail (Phase 10b-1 Task 4, R-118). The ONE closed authority
# for email verification, password reset, identity-deletion recovery and (from
# 10b-2) local-factor enrollment and workspace invites. Both unset = no mail is
# sent anywhere: sign-up and reset still work, and the dev console prints the
# link in development only (never elsewhere). Both set = every send is quota
# admitted (compiled 80/day, 2,400/month; invites 50/day, 1,500/month, R-125) and
# recorded in `auth_mail_outbox` before Resend is called.
# API key: resend.com → API Keys. FROM must be a sender on a verified domain,
# e.g. `Respin <account@your-verified-domain>`. Never commit either.
# T-16 (real controlled delivery through a live Resend account) remains
# separate acceptance evidence; nothing here is that evidence.
RESEND_API_KEY=
RESEND_FROM=

# --- Support channel (audit P6-R6 amendment; decisions R-176, plan label R-159)
# ONE address an operator reads. When it is set, every refusal whose remedy ends
# with a person names it ("If it keeps happening, email <address>."), as do the
# /usage unsettled-charge note and /api/export's failure body. When it is unset,
# blank or malformed, NO screen promises contact at all: the copy keeps only the
# remedy the creator can act on. Read on the server only, in
# app/support-contact.ts. Never set an address nobody monitors.
RESPIN_SUPPORT_EMAIL=

# --- Operator scripts: scripts/backup.sh and scripts/restore-drill.sh (R-155).
# These are NOT app settings — never put them in .env.local. They are set in
# the operator's own shell for one run; RUNBOOK.md (Respin → Restore drill)
# has every command with its inputs. Listed here because this file is the
# env authority.
#
# backup.sh — required: DATABASE_URL (above; the database to back up), plus
# BACKUP_DIR=           # storage independent of the database host
# BACKUP_PASSPHRASE_FILE=   # the AES256 passphrase; stored off-host (T-23)
# RETENTION_DAYS=       # optional, default and maximum 21 (R-119)
# and RESPIN_DELETION_JOURNAL_BUCKET / _ENVIRONMENT (above), which the manifest
# records; restore-drill.sh then refuses to verify that backup against any other
# journal. Without them the manifest is a legacy one (decisions.md R-155 §8).
#
# restore-drill.sh — required:
# BACKUP_FILE=          # .../respin-<stamp>.dump.gz.gpg; its manifest beside it
# MAINTENANCE_URL=      # a maintenance database on the restore target server
# RESTORE_SERVING_DISABLED=   # exactly `confirmed`, after stopping app and workers
# DRILL_DB=             # optional, default respin_restore_drill (name guarded)
# plus BACKUP_PASSPHRASE_FILE and the three RESPIN_DELETION_JOURNAL_* names.

# There is deliberately no YouTube variable consumed by this build. Live
# YouTube metadata/quota evidence is deferred as T-15; until its adapter and
# acceptance walk exist, scheduled refresh returns
# `youtube_live_discovery_unverified` and no digest schedule is created.
